For a polygon to be convex, every interior angle must be strictly less than 180 degrees, so I test the sign of the cross product of every pair of consecutive edge direction vectors from a single point. If the sign ever changes, the turn between edges reverses direction and the polygon is not convex. The same point can be considered as the tail of every edge, so each point starts an n - 2 tests. To stop a collinear input from slipping through, I include any vertex that lies on the same line through two of its neighbours; they never yield anything other than a perpendicular edge, and checking every interior point against them rules out a straight boundary for fewer tests than a full check of all triples.

None of the example shapes appears in the body, and the check that edges must turn in the same direction is standard for polygons given with a consistent winding order, which the spec requires. The first argument examples each have one segment meeting at a sharp interior corner, and槛 a crossover case is a house with a duplicate corner, such as [(0, 0), (0, 1), (0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1)], whose tail's cross product with the right wall cancels the other chord's sign and the result is False. A straight triangle with empty sign flips returns False as well, matching the note that collinear polygons are not counted.

