The polygon is convex if and only if every interior angle is less than 180 degrees, which means that consecutive turns all go in the same direction. I look at each triple of consecutive vertices as the three points a, b and c, and compute the sign of the z-component of the cross product of ab and ac. Its sign tells whether the turn from ab to ac is a left turn or a right turn. I also compare it to the turn just before a, and when the signs differ that means the polygon bends inward. If none of the consecutive triples produces a sign change, the whole polygon turns in one direction and it is convex; a list of three collinear points gives only one triple with a zero cross product and returns True. For the sign test I use less than rather than at-most because a zero cross product comes from collinear points and the task excludes polygons where all vertices are collinear.

Each vertex is compared with its immediate neighbour and with its other two adjacent neighbours via slicing with modulo 2, and every contiguous triple is used exactly once. The comparisons depend only on dot products of adjacent edges, so the function is exact for any set of polygonal points and is not limited to those in the example calls.

Verdict: An exact convexity test built from vector signs.

